this virus has stolen so much and many  who've lost loved ones haven't even been  able to say goodbye  8 news now reporter madison kimbrough  spoke to a psychologist and a man who  lost his mother to the coronavirus  she joins us live in the studio now with  more madison yes while our emotions are  not something we often publicly discuss  during time of isolation due to the  cova-19 pandemic  it's important we find a way to cope  i've seen people continue to use drugs  even more than they did before  feel isolated i see people losing family  members and friends  and the grief part that you mentioned is  about a loss of how life used to be  a reality for many this past year as  covet 19 has taken over  our lives whether you know someone that  has had it or you've experienced it  yourself  the lack of normalcy we have endured for  the past 10 months would make anyone  feel  out of control dr solomon says lately  the amount of drug use  she has seen at her recovery center has  been extremely high  a lot of people coming to me because  they have substance abuse issues  that has gotten so out of hand because  they're just home all the time and so  there's no real delineation between work  time and home time  patrick casal a health insurance broker  in las vegas recently just lost his  mother to the deadly virus  he says the hardest part was not being  able to be with her when she took her  last breath  it's the hardest thing i've ever had to  do in my life not to say goodbye to my  mother i hold her  i tell her i love her i can't believe  how many people i actually know  that either had coveted or passed away  from covid dr  solomon says normalcy and connectiveness  is key when coping with this kind of  grief  whatever makes you feel normal so trying  to figure out how you can do certain  activities whether they're exercise  connecting with friends over zoom over  face time however you can feel connected  to family and friends  is so important right now as for casal  in march he plans on having a ceremony  in his mother's honor  and plans to spread her ashes in her  home state of new york  now if you or you know someone currently  having a hard time adjusting to the  changes  brought on by the pandemic you're not  alone and there are resources to help  the national suicide prevention line is  up on your screen  reporting in studio madison kimbrough 8  news now
 

